AD  - Department of Computer Applications, Agurchand Manmull Jain College, India
AB  - Urbanization has greatly contributed to the exposure to floods in urban areas, and as such, it requires precise and readable hyperlocal flood susceptibility maps. The research paper suggests the XGeoFlood-XAI, a hybrid GeoAI framework that integrates convolutional neural networks, graph neural networks, and transformer-based spatial attention based on high-resolution urban flood evaluation and expects elicitable AI processing. Multimodal geospatial such as digital elevation models, rainfall intensity and soil properties, drainage connectivity and impervious surface indices are reconciled and converted to enriched features of flood-conditioning. The hybrid model is able to capture both the local terrain properties as well as long-range drainage properties and probabilistic calibration module provides uncertainty-informed predictions. The model had an accuracy of 96.3, F1-score 0.96 and AUC 0.98 which was better compared to traditional machine learning models and individual deep learning models. Spatial SHAP-based attribution additionally determines rainfall intensity and ratio of impervious surfaces as the major drivers of floods in fast urbanizing neighborhoods. GeoAI and XAI are designed to help in the openness of their use, which guarantees increased predictive performance as well as transparency to allow data-driven decisions in the urban planning process. The suggested framework offers a scalable, interpretable and decision ready solution to hyperlocal urban flood susceptibility mapping in dynamic urban settings. © 2026 IEEE.
