AB  - The predominant urban form and a significant spatial planning challenge in recent times is the peri-urban phenomenon. In advanced industrialized nations, per-urban areas undergo social, economic, and spatial transformations, while in developing countries, they often experience disorderly urbanization leading to urban sprawl. Changes in land use and land cover (LULC) driven by human activities are crucial factors in the growth and development of urban environments. The objective of this research is to identify modifications in LULC patterns in both urban and surrounding suburban regions, with a projection of these patterns into the year 2030. The methodology incorporates the analysis of Landsat satellite data, utilizing both the Markov Chain and Cellular Automata (MC-CA) models, alongside the Artificial Neural Network-Multilayer Perceptron (ANN-MLP) models. The study focuses on monitoring the developmental trajectory of Shahrekord city in Iran using Landsat remote sensing data spanning from 1985 to 2015. The research employs the Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) and CA-Markov methodologies for training and validation across two time spans: 1985–2000 and 2000–2015. Parameters and influential factors, including proximity to roads, academic institutions, and industrial areas, are considered drivers of growth and development. The combined application of MLP and CA-Markov yields more accurate outcomes. The results demonstrate shifts in urban land use by 2030, as follows: urban green space will increase from 537 to 590 ha, built-up areas will expand from 2168 to 3143 ha, agricultural lands will decrease from 4327 to 3908 ha, and barren land will shrink from 1576 to 968 ha. This geospatial artificial intelligence model accurately forecasts urban and peri-urban changes. Such land change modeling holds the potential to assist decision makers in addressing issues related to urban sprawl. © 2025 Elsevier Inc.
