TI  - Comparative Study Integrating the Vulnerability to Forest Fire and Landslide Using GIS and Python Based Approach
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of 2025 10th International Conference on Science Technology, Engineering and Mathematics, ICONSTEM 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICONSTEM65670.2025.11374424
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034471028?origin=resultslist
AD  - Saveetha School of Engineering, SIMATS, Department of Civil Engineering, Chennai, India
AB  - Owing to its precipitous slopes, heavy monsoon rainfall, and increasing human encroachment, the Nilgiris district situated in Tamil Nadu's Western Ghats is susceptible to frequent landslides and wildfires. Utilizing GIS and Python-based analytical methods, this preliminary investigation offers a comparative assessment of landslide and forest fire susceptibility. Datasets from MODIS fire sites, SRTM DEM, Landsat8/9, and rainfall correlation models were used to provide slope, NDVI, and elevation features. Python module based rasterio, Numpy, Scikit-learn was used to analyze and assess the normalized raster layers to provide the initial susceptibility indicators. An elevation-slope correction value of r=0.46 and a vegetation-slope correlation coefficient of r=0.68 were found by statistical analysis, suggesting that topographic and vegetation stress factors are important in the occurrence of the hazards. According to the thermal response, roughly 53.1% of the Nilgiris as per the study area is classified as having fire risk and 67.9% of the territory is classified as being in landslide susceptibility zone. The results show how Geo-AI based spatial modelling may enhance risk management and encourage sustainable land-use planning in mountainous areas, while also offering preliminary insights into multihazard interactions in the Nilgiris. © 2025 IEEE.
