AB  - The rapid advancement of Geospatial Artificial Intelligence (GeoAI) has transformed Earth Observation (EO) data analysis by enabling automated, high-precision decision-making in domains such as environmental monitoring, urban planning, and climate change mitigation. However, the complexity of deep learning models used in GeoAI raises critical concerns about interpretability and accountability. This chapter introduces the foundations of Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) in the geospatial domain, outlining key challenges associated with interpreting complex models trained on multimodal geospatial data. It discusses the need for explainability in tasks such as land cover classification, hyperspectral unmixing, and mobility optimization, where model transparency is essential for ensuring reliability. The chapter categorizes XAI techniques into ante-hoc and post-hoc approaches, highlighting their applicability in geospatial workflows. Ante-hoc methods provide interpretability, including physics-informed neural networks (PINNs), symbolic regression, and Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks (KANs). At the same time, post-hoc approaches such as Shapley values, gradient-based saliency maps, and counterfactual reasoning are used to explain the behavior of black-box models. This chapter concludes by emphasizing the growing importance of explainability in geospatial AI for operational transparency, setting the stage for subsequent chapters that delve deeper into state-of-the-art XAI methodologies. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Arun PV, Jocelyn Chanussot, B Krishna Mohan, D Nagesh Kumar, and Alok Porwal; individual chapters, the contributors.
