AB  - Geospatial technology, including remote sensing, plays an important role in understanding climate systems and taking necessary measures toward climate change adaptation. It enables the monitoring of the earth's resources at different spatiotemporal scales to reduce the impact of climate change. This chapter discusses the integration of geospatial technology with artificial intelligence (AI) to manage earth resources effectively. A novel open-source framework that leverages a web-based geographic information system (WebGIS) application integrated with AI capabilities is introduced, detailing the technological advances, methodologies, and practical applications in earth resource management. The comprehensive full stack application is designed to streamline object detection, instance segmentation, and model training on custom datasets. By uniquely integrating available tile satellite image services, the application offers a robust and versatile platform for satellite imagery analysis. Key features include the ability for users to select an area of interest on the map or run inference on the visible map area, download shapefiles of detected features for seamless use and integration with other GIS tools, generate inference on user uploaded data, and visualize results directly on the map. The application’s built in model training capability supports user uploaded datasets, allowing configuration of training parameters and the option to train models from scratch or fine tune pretrained models. This democratizes access to advanced AI techniques and customized tools to address specific user needs. Additionally, the application includes user-wise mode and storage capabilities, ensuring personalized and persistent data management. By combining powerful geospatial functionalities with advanced machine learning workflows, the proposed web based open source framework addresses the growing demand for accessible, high performance GeoAI tools in environmental monitoring, urban planning, and disaster management, empowering users to derive meaningful insights from complex geospatial data. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved..
