AB  - The integration of urban science with spatial artificial intelligence, referred to as Urban-AI or Geo-AI, is playing an increasingly important role in monitoring cities, supporting urban planning, and addressing urban challenges. In this context, investigating how cities are perceived by individuals and understanding the effects of urban environments on people have emerged as important research topics that can directly influence individuals’ quality of life. In recent years, street-level imagery (SLI) has been widely used to evaluate urban perception through artificial intelligence-based algorithms. In particular, generative artificial intelligence offers growing potential for analyzing urban environments and supporting the development of spatial solutions. However, ethical risks such as algorithmic bias, socio-economic inequalities, and data representativeness issues remain important concerns. Therefore, before enabling generative AI-based systems to support transformations in urban environments, it is critical to investigate their functionality and development from a spatial data perspective. This study presents an empirical framework to evaluate the usability of crowdsourced SLI for urban perception research. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.
