TY  - CONF
AU  - Thamilarasi, V.
AU  - Roselin, R.
AU  - Sivakumar, J.
TI  - Geospatial AI for Intelligent Urban Heat Island Detection and Mitigation
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Sustainable Engineering and Digital Innovation, ICSEDI 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ICSEDI66420.2026.11568360
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044131883?origin=resultslist
AD  - Sri Sarada College for Women (Autonomous), Department of Computer Science, Tamilnadu, Salem, India
AD  - Global College of Engineering and Technology, Computer Science and Creative Technology, Muscat, Oman
AB  - The higher temperatures that metropolitan regions typically experience when compared to their rural surroundings are known as the metropolitan Heat Island (UHI) effect. Cities are becoming heat traps due to rapid urbanization, which is raising environmental issues. Increasing urbanization, heat-retaining infrastructure, and a decline in green space are the main causes of urban heat islands (UHIs), a major environmental and public health concern in contemporary cities. The accuracy and scalability of traditional approaches to UHI detection and mitigation are constrained. By combining GIS, remote sensing, and AI-based analytics, Geospatial Artificial Intelligence (GeoAI) offers novel ways to track, forecast, and lessen the effects of UHI. With an emphasis on sustainable and climate-resilient urban planning, this article explores the role of GeoAI in high-resolution UHI mapping, predictive modelling, and mitigation strategy design. In order to address this pressing issue, this research provides a remotely sensing-based, globally scalable technology for UHI identification. The technique combines multidimensional spatial, environmental, and socio-urban aspects with satellite-derived data to enable thorough UHI analysis anywhere in the world. This study proposes a GeoAI framework that leverages high-resolution satellite imagery, climate data, and urban morphology features to detect UHI hotspots with high accuracy and interpretability. The approach employs deep learning and spatial analysis techniques to map heat distribution patterns, identify critical urban zones affected by thermal anomalies, and explore the role of land use/land cover, vegetation indices, and built-up density in UHI formation. Furthermore, the study introduces predictive modeling for UHI mitigation by evaluating potential cooling strategies such as urban greening, reflective surfaces, and water-based interventions. The findings aim to support urban planners, policymakers, and environmental agencies in developing sustainable, climate-resilient cities through actionable, geospatially informed decision-making  © 2026 IEEE.
